{
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/tsconfig",

  /*
   * T-27. Every `tsconfig.json` in this repository excludes `src/**\/*.test.ts`,
   * so until this file existed NO test file was ever type-checked. A fixture
   * could drift from a changed type and `tsc --build` stayed green — which is
   * exactly what happened in T-04, where a required field was added to
   * `CatalogReleaseManifest` and every fixture silently lacked it until the
   * tests ran.
   *
   * One root project rather than fifteen per-package ones, deliberately: the
   * include list below mirrors `vitest.config.ts`'s own `test.include`, so the
   * set of files that RUN and the set that are CHECKED come from the same
   * shape. Two lists that could drift apart is the defect this repository keeps
   * finding in itself.
   *
   * BLIND SPOTS, stated in the same breath as the guarantee (house rule):
   *   - This project does not emit. It cannot catch an error that only appears
   *     in declaration emit; `tsc --build` still owns that.
   *   - It resolves `@rms/*` through `paths` to SOURCE, not to built `.d.ts`.
   *     That is what makes fixture drift visible immediately, but it means a
   *     package's public surface is checked as written rather than as shipped.
   *   - It checks the files vitest is configured to run. A test file outside
   *     that glob is invisible to both, and neither would say so.
   */
  "extends": "./tsconfig.base.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "noEmit": true,
    "composite": false,
    "declaration": false,
    "declarationMap": false,
    "sourceMap": false,
    "types": ["node"],
    /*
     * S1. `lib.dom` is here because `apps/studio-web/src/app.test.tsx` is a
     * component test and genuinely needs `document`. F-43's lesson was that the
     * type gate must be able to SEE `.tsx`; being able to see it and not being
     * able to check it is the same hole one step further in.
     *
     * THE WIDENING, stated rather than discovered later: every test file in the
     * repository can now reference `document` and `window` and will type-check.
     * A kernel test that did so would be wrong. Two things bound it — the
     * purity scan (`check-boundaries`) covers kernel SOURCE, which is where the
     * invariant actually lives, and `vitest.config.ts` sets `environment: 'node'`
     * globally, so a kernel test touching the DOM fails at run time rather than
     * passing quietly. Neither is a type-level guard, and there is none.
     */
    "lib": ["ES2022", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"]
  },
  "include": [
    "packages/**/*.test.ts",
    "packages/**/*.test.tsx",
    "apps/**/*.test.ts",
    "apps/**/*.test.tsx",
    "tools/**/*.test.ts",
    "vitest.alias.ts"
  ]
}
